  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\n   We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by representing candidate solutions (step functions on [0,2]) via piecewise constant interpolations on uniformly discrete grids. The core optimization targets the maximum overlap correlation, equivalent to minimizing the maximum cross-correlation with its complement. Our method systematically:\n\n   - Uses a structured initialization (combining parent vector with golden-angle seeding for disparate patterns)\n   - Performs multi-scale optimization across grid resolutions (200, 250, 300 points)\n   - Employs directed local search via structured swaps (adjacent, distant, frame-aligned) and interpolation guidance\n   - Combines these analytical moves with gradient-based polishing and binary anchoring\n\n   We target improvements by reducing the \"ringing\" effect observed in discreteshift confilicts, increasing anti-correlation near peaks, and reprojection of suboptimal regions. Configuration ramping (from 200 to 300 points) allows leveraging increased resolution for candidate refinement.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\n   Variables:\n     - n: grid size (200, 250, or 300)\n     - h: piecewise constant approximant to step function, length n, elements in [0,1], sum= n/2\n     - dx = 2.0 / n\n\n   Objective:\n     C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \n        = dx * max_lag { np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[lag] }\n\n   Constraints:\n     Each h[i] \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h[i]*dx = 1\n\n   Our algorithm:\n     - Projects highest (from parent) or random generated to grid (if grid changes)\n     - Enforces feasibility by mixing parent parts with seed content\n     - Tracks multiple time-limited candidates\n     - Uses layered moves (binary swaps, linear interpolation) guided by lag landscape\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\n   a. Initialization and validation:\n        - On grid size n (200, 250, 300), verify initial_h array is float32\n        - Compute and store shifted parent representation via interpolate-fit (if using parent)\n        - PyTorch/TensorFlow is not used (only numpy), but state vector as changes\n        - Function interface matches: run(seed, budget_s) returning (h_values, c5_bound, n)\n\n   b. Phase 0: Evaluation functions\n        - Compute_c5(h, n): uses correlate + dx scaling\n        - Project_feasible(h, n): normalize-sum with clipping (0-1)\n\n   c. Phase 1: Candidate generation (grid-independent)\n        - multi_start(max_starts=8, seed=None) to generate starting points:\n            - Initial candidates either parent-projected or seeded goldens (using golden ratio 1.618)\n        - Represent candidates as periodic tile of a window: h_win length=min(50,4*n)\n\n   d. Phase 2: Multi-resolution optimization\n        Grids: tried [200,250,300] (preparation, then optimization)\n        If grid change during optimization, convert to new dimension via:\n            - Local regulation hierarchical cell adjustment (particle-scale)\n            - Ensure sum constraint adjusted proportionally (2*n_chosen/sum_old)\n\n   e. Phase 3: Local search by structured mutations\n        Moves supported:\n            - Rearrangement swaps (local index \u00b11)\n            - Golden-aligned swaps (distance related to golden ratio)\n            - Frame-aligned (period boundary driven swaps)\n            - Interpolation-guided transformation (into parent or other representation)\n\n   f. Phase 4: Specialized local search\n        - Pattern anti-correlation maximization via interpolation back to parent\n        - Using finite-differences to compute gradient and trust-region updates\n\n   g. Phase 5: Progressive time budget\n        - 10% for grid preparation\n        - 60% for local search\n        - 30% for gradient-based polishing\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n   Parameter setup:\n        - Num_candidates = 8\n        - Grid defaults: [200,250,300]\n        - Lag horizon: 3-5 most significant (by absolute correlation value)\n        - Core optimization steps (per candidate):\n            - 75% swaps\n            - 10% parameter.Clamp (0,1)\n            - 15% interpolation-distortion\n\n   Variations to try:\n        - Migrate_from(parent, h):   replace randomly 25% of elements with parent values.\n        - Golden_layered(start, n): produces candidate by golden spiral of particles\n        - Local_Binary_Swap(cnd, limit=1000): swaps locally-seeking indices without fixed pattern.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n   Estimate:\n        - 200 points: ~75ms simple move, 1100s allows ~150 such function evaluations\n        - We use candidate-progression tree: 4 root candidates, each with 4 branches -> 64 total points\n        - Total operations: ~50 (per candidate) * 64 * 10 = \n            -> with 1100 seconds budget, we aim for ~5-6 such independent paths.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\n   - Final candidate evaluated with exact correlate method\n   - Binary checks: all h[i] \u2208 [0,1], sum with dx==(1+\u03b5)\n   - Output caught block keeps only improvements and runs within budget\n   - Deadlines enforced by time-check in each optimization cycle.\n\n   Multiple candidate paths allowed to ensure best time-\u7528\u4e8e within the budget. Return after deadline.",
